 "characters": 874,
 "text": "Artemis Two's flight took ten days: launch on April first, a seven-hour lunar flyby on April sixth, splashdown off California on April tenth. But by then the plan for the next mission had already changed. On February twenty-seventh, NASA rewrote the order of the Artemis program. Artemis Three had been the landing. It became a test flight in low Earth orbit, set for twenty twenty-seven, and the first landing moved to Artemis Four, targeted for twenty twenty-eight. NASA also promised at least one landing every year after that. NASA's reasoning, in its own announcement: keep the rocket and spacecraft as close as possible to the version that already flew, fly more often, and build up step by step, with each mission big enough to make progress but not so big that it takes unnecessary risk. NASA's administrator said that's how the agency did it in nineteen sixty-nine.",
 "text_sha256": "5261a81563abcccdf3277c75d8537c0cf58eb0b394a86dcee74a8fe6aa4faef1",
